1
00:00:04,500 --> 00:00:08,356
The release script stamps
every build with a commit ID.

2
00:00:08,396 --> 00:00:12,394
It gets that ID by reading the
file .git/refs/heads/main.

3
00:00:12,434 --> 00:00:18,133
It has done so for years. On the new build
image that file doesn't exist, and the

4
00:00:18,173 --> 00:00:19,125
release stops.

5
00:00:19,465 --> 00:00:22,061
Your CTO asks: is the repository damaged?

6
00:00:22,401 --> 00:00:27,580
It isn't. The branch exists and Git resolves
it. The ref is stored somewhere else,

7
00:00:27,620 --> 00:00:32,925
and the script should have asked Git. A ref
is a name that holds an object ID, and a

8
00:00:32,965 --> 00:00:38,144
branch is one kind of ref. This video shows
every place a ref can be stored in the

9
00:00:38,184 --> 00:00:41,835
default format, and why no
script should read any of them.

10
00:00:41,875 --> 00:00:44,762
Keep that missing file
in mind. It comes back.

11
00:00:46,602 --> 00:00:50,372
Welcome back to Git and GitHub
Deep Mastery. Pull up a chair.

12
00:00:50,412 --> 00:00:55,556
You've used refs since the first part of
this course: a branch is a ref, a tag is a

13
00:00:55,596 --> 00:00:58,991
ref, HEAD is a ref. Now we
look at how they are stored.

14
00:00:59,331 --> 00:01:05,006
Three sections of Chapter 3. First, refs
under refs/ as loose files and as lines of

15
00:01:05,046 --> 00:01:10,789
packed-refs. Second, the refs that live
outside refs/, called root refs, and the two

16
00:01:10,829 --> 00:01:16,435
pseudorefs. Third, where reflogs are kept.
A reflog is the journal of the values a

17
00:01:16,475 --> 00:01:17,261
ref has had.

18
00:01:17,601 --> 00:01:25,433
Three replays from labs/ch03: refs-storage.sh,
root-refs.sh, and the replay of Lab

19
00:01:25,473 --> 00:01:27,928
17.3 up to its checkpoint.

20
00:01:28,268 --> 00:01:31,458
The labels, from the chapter's
command safety table.

21
00:01:31,498 --> 00:01:36,552
git for-each-ref and git show-ref are 🟢
SAFE. git pack-refs --all is 🟢: it changes

22
00:01:36,592 --> 00:01:41,272
the storage of refs, not their content.
git update-ref with a new value is 🟡

23
00:01:41,312 --> 00:01:46,179
CAUTION, and so is git symbolic-ref. git
update-ref -d is 🔴 DANGEROUS, and I'll

24
00:01:46,219 --> 00:01:48,788
answer the five questions when it appears.

25
00:01:50,628 --> 00:01:52,040
After this video you can:

26
00:01:52,380 --> 00:01:56,743
Explain why a branch may have no file
under refs/heads/ and still exist.

27
00:01:57,083 --> 00:02:01,597
Create, update, verify and delete refs
with plumbing, with a reflog message.

28
00:02:01,937 --> 00:02:06,014
Name the root refs and the pseudorefs
and say which command writes each.

29
00:02:06,354 --> 00:02:09,435
Say which of them protect
objects from garbage collection.

30
00:02:09,775 --> 00:02:12,799
Say where reflogs are stored
and when a ref has none.

31
00:02:14,639 --> 00:02:20,286
In one sentence: a ref is a name that holds
an object ID or the name of another ref,

32
00:02:20,326 --> 00:02:25,432
and in the default files format a ref
is a small file under refs/, a line in

33
00:02:25,472 --> 00:02:26,853
packed-refs, or both.

34
00:02:27,193 --> 00:02:32,214
The glossary's definition: "a name that
points to an object name or another ref (the

35
00:02:32,254 --> 00:02:37,214
latter is called a symbolic ref)". Names
either start with refs/ or sit in the root

36
00:02:37,254 --> 00:02:38,238
of the hierarchy.

37
00:02:38,578 --> 00:02:43,497
Under refs/: refs/heads/<name> is a local
branch, moved by commit, merge, reset,

38
00:02:43,537 --> 00:02:48,518
rebase and git branch -f. refs/tags/<name>
is a tag, pointing at a commit or at a

39
00:02:48,558 --> 00:02:53,477
tag object, meant to stay put.
refs/remotes/<remote>/<name> is a remote-tracking

40
00:02:53,517 --> 00:02:58,684
branch, the last known position of a branch
in that remote, moved by fetch and push.

41
00:02:58,724 --> 00:03:03,829
refs/stash is the newest stash entry, work
you set aside, and older entries live in

42
00:03:03,869 --> 00:03:08,416
its reflog. And tools own namespaces
such as refs/bisect/, refs/notes/ and

43
00:03:08,456 --> 00:03:09,284
refs/replace/.

44
00:03:09,624 --> 00:03:14,965
How they're stored in the files format. A
loose ref is a file holding the ID and a

45
00:03:15,005 --> 00:03:18,771
newline. A symbolic ref is a
file holding ref: and a name.

46
00:03:18,811 --> 00:03:22,118
packed-refs is one sorted
text file with many refs.

47
00:03:22,158 --> 00:03:27,499
Why three forms? One file per ref wastes
space and is slow with thousands of refs.

48
00:03:27,839 --> 00:03:32,905
The lookup rule is the heart of this video.
Lookup reads the loose file first and

49
00:03:32,945 --> 00:03:38,201
packed-refs second. And, quoting the manual
of git pack-refs: "subsequent updates to

50
00:03:38,241 --> 00:03:41,605
branches always create new
files under $GIT_DIR/refs".

51
00:03:41,645 --> 00:03:46,774
So after packing, a branch that moves gets
a loose file again, and the old line in

52
00:03:46,814 --> 00:03:49,674
packed-refs stays, stale.
The loose file wins.

53
00:03:50,014 --> 00:03:54,901
The plumbing works the same in every
format. git for-each-ref lists and formats.

54
00:03:54,941 --> 00:03:59,890
git show-ref lists and verifies. git
update-ref writes or deletes one ref with an

55
00:03:59,930 --> 00:04:04,878
optional check of its old value. git
symbolic-ref reads and writes symbolic refs.

56
00:04:04,918 --> 00:04:09,374
git pack-refs moves loose refs into
packed-refs without changing a value.

57
00:04:09,714 --> 00:04:14,897
Second topic: root refs. In one sentence:
a few refs live directly in the repository

58
00:04:14,937 --> 00:04:18,565
directory, with names in capital
letters that end in _HEAD.

59
00:04:18,605 --> 00:04:23,663
Two of them, FETCH_HEAD and MERGE_HEAD,
aren't refs at all in the strict sense and

60
00:04:23,703 --> 00:04:25,031
are called pseudorefs.

61
00:04:25,371 --> 00:04:29,996
The glossary says a pseudoref has "different
semantics than normal refs", can be

62
00:04:30,036 --> 00:04:34,837
read by normal Git commands, and cannot be
written by commands like git update-ref.

63
00:04:34,877 --> 00:04:39,736
It lists two. FETCH_HEAD "may refer to
multiple object IDs", each annotated with its

64
00:04:39,776 --> 00:04:43,701
source. MERGE_HEAD "contains all
commit IDs which are being merged".

65
00:04:44,041 --> 00:04:47,498
A version note you need when
you read older material.

66
00:04:47,538 --> 00:04:52,644
Before Git 2.46 the glossary called every
special file under .git a pseudoref.

67
00:04:52,684 --> 00:04:56,932
Since Git 2.46, "pseudoref" means
FETCH_HEAD and MERGE_HEAD only.

68
00:04:56,972 --> 00:05:01,617
The others are root refs, stored by
the ref backend like any other ref.

69
00:05:01,657 --> 00:05:07,159
Say "root ref" for ORIG_HEAD and its relatives,
and notice that older tutorials, and

70
00:05:07,199 --> 00:05:12,701
the description of --include-root-refs in
the git for-each-ref manual, still use the

71
00:05:12,741 --> 00:05:13,492
old wording.

72
00:05:13,832 --> 00:05:19,131
HEAD is written by git init, git switch
and git checkout, and is never gone.

73
00:05:19,171 --> 00:05:25,032
ORIG_HEAD is written by merge, rebase, reset
and git am, and on Git 2.55 also by git

74
00:05:25,072 --> 00:05:30,792
stash push. It holds where HEAD was before,
until the next such command overwrites

75
00:05:30,832 --> 00:05:31,003
it.

76
00:05:31,343 --> 00:05:35,587
FETCH_HEAD is written by fetch and pull.
MERGE_HEAD is written by a merge that

77
00:05:35,627 --> 00:05:38,607
stops, and goes when the merge
is committed or aborted.

78
00:05:38,647 --> 00:05:43,111
CHERRY_PICK_HEAD and REVERT_HEAD are written
by a cherry-pick or revert that stops

79
00:05:43,151 --> 00:05:44,484
or runs with --no-commit.

80
00:05:44,824 --> 00:05:50,847
REBASE_HEAD is written by a rebase that
stops. BISECT_HEAD is written by git bisect

81
00:05:50,887 --> 00:05:56,545
--no-checkout. AUTO_MERGE is written by
the ort merge machinery when it leaves

82
00:05:56,585 --> 00:06:00,709
conflicts, and on Git 2.55
also by a clean git stash pop.

83
00:06:00,749 --> 00:06:01,878
It holds a tree.

84
00:06:02,218 --> 00:06:06,618
The property that matters in an incident:
these names, HEAD excepted, aren't

85
00:06:06,658 --> 00:06:11,525
starting points for reachability. An object
is reachable when a starting point leads

86
00:06:11,565 --> 00:06:14,972
to it, and garbage collection
deletes what nothing reaches.

87
00:06:15,012 --> 00:06:18,653
The starting points are refs/,
HEAD, the reflogs and the index.

88
00:06:18,693 --> 00:06:23,268
So FETCH_HEAD, ORIG_HEAD and AUTO_MERGE
protect nothing from garbage collection

89
00:06:23,308 --> 00:06:24,670
beyond the grace period.

90
00:06:25,010 --> 00:06:30,395
Third topic: reflogs. In one sentence: a
reflog is an append-only record of the

91
00:06:30,435 --> 00:06:35,819
values a ref has had, one file per ref
under logs/ in the files format, written

92
00:06:35,859 --> 00:06:41,587
whenever the ref moves. core.logAllRefUpdates
is true in a repository with a working

93
00:06:41,627 --> 00:06:47,079
tree, which creates logs for HEAD, branches,
remote-tracking branches and notes.

94
00:06:47,419 --> 00:06:50,855
The value always extends this
to every ref under refs/.

95
00:06:50,895 --> 00:06:55,973
A bare repository, one without a working
tree, has it off and keeps no reflogs by

96
00:06:56,013 --> 00:07:01,281
default. Tags get no reflog under the default
setting, and deleting a branch deletes

97
00:07:01,321 --> 00:07:01,786
its log.

98
00:07:02,126 --> 00:07:06,873
When should you not use the plumbing? git
update-ref on the checked-out branch and

99
00:07:06,913 --> 00:07:11,425
git symbolic-ref HEAD move the branch
and leave the index and the working tree

100
00:07:11,465 --> 00:07:15,803
behind. Treat them as repairs. Use git
switch and git reset for daily work.

101
00:07:17,643 --> 00:07:23,131
The textbook's analogy is signposts. Each
carries a name and points at one house, an

102
00:07:23,171 --> 00:07:27,409
object. A few point at another
signpost: those are symbolic refs.

103
00:07:27,449 --> 00:07:30,568
Moving a signpost is cheap
and changes no house.

104
00:07:30,908 --> 00:07:36,105
The analogy breaks at demolition. In Git,
a house that no signpost leads to is

105
00:07:36,145 --> 00:07:39,462
eventually torn down. That
was the previous video.

106
00:07:39,802 --> 00:07:44,408
Add one thing to the picture for this video:
the signposts are kept in more than one

107
00:07:44,448 --> 00:07:48,889
cupboard. You don't open the cupboards
yourself. You ask the keeper, which is the

108
00:07:48,929 --> 00:07:53,481
plumbing, Git's low-level commands, and the
keeper knows which cupboard is current.

109
00:07:55,321 --> 00:07:59,905
Loose file first. If there is one, it's
the answer, whatever packed-refs says.

110
00:07:59,945 --> 00:08:03,463
Only if there is none does Git
read the line in packed-refs.

111
00:08:03,503 --> 00:08:07,198
A script that reads only the file
fails when the ref is packed.

112
00:08:07,238 --> 00:08:11,823
A script that reads only packed-refs gets
a stale value when the ref has moved

113
00:08:11,863 --> 00:08:12,179
since.

114
00:08:12,519 --> 00:08:17,049
The old value, the new value, the
committer's identity and time, a tab, and a

115
00:08:17,089 --> 00:08:18,652
message naming the command.

116
00:08:20,492 --> 00:08:25,455
The inference-service repository again,
now with a remote, a stash entry and two

117
00:08:25,495 --> 00:08:25,768
tags.

118
00:08:26,108 --> 00:08:31,778
Seven files. Five hold an object ID.
Two hold ref: and a name: those are the

119
00:08:31,818 --> 00:08:32,829
symbolic refs.

120
00:08:33,169 --> 00:08:39,329
for-each-ref prints ID, type and full
name by default, and --format chooses any

121
00:08:39,369 --> 00:08:45,920
field. show-ref --dereference adds a ^{}
line with the commit an annotated tag peels

122
00:08:45,960 --> 00:08:50,942
to. --verify takes a full name.
--exists answers by exit status.

123
00:08:51,282 --> 00:08:56,290
Try it now, thirty seconds, in a repository
in the lab shell: run git for-each-ref

124
00:08:56,330 --> 00:08:58,198
and count the lines. I'll wait.

125
00:09:03,038 --> 00:09:06,231
Each line is one ref: an
ID, a type and a full name.

126
00:09:06,271 --> 00:09:09,091
Your branches are the
lines under refs/heads/.

127
00:09:09,431 --> 00:09:13,302
Now 🟡: git update-ref. It
changes one ref and its reflog.

128
00:09:13,342 --> 00:09:18,036
Preview with git rev-parse on the
ref. Recover with the ref's reflog.

129
00:09:18,376 --> 00:09:23,048
On the graph, that's a new name. The demo
creates the ref experiment at the release

130
00:09:23,088 --> 00:09:23,615
candidate.

131
00:09:23,955 --> 00:09:27,855
Then it moves the name to the
commit of main. No commit changes.

132
00:09:28,195 --> 00:09:31,837
The third command has a third
argument: an expected old value.

133
00:09:31,877 --> 00:09:36,587
The branch is at the release candidate,
and the command expects the root commit.

134
00:09:36,627 --> 00:09:39,022
What happens? Say it out loud. I'll wait.

135
00:09:43,862 --> 00:09:47,769
It's refused: "is at 4f2cc0c...
but expected b602c1f...".

136
00:09:47,809 --> 00:09:51,648
With a third argument, update-ref
is a compare-and-swap.

137
00:09:51,688 --> 00:09:57,188
The update happens only if the ref still
has the expected old value, which keeps

138
00:09:57,228 --> 00:10:02,521
concurrent writers from overwriting each
other. It's the idea behind git push

139
00:10:02,561 --> 00:10:07,993
--force-with-lease. Two more refusals
follow: an ID that names no object, and a

140
00:10:08,033 --> 00:10:10,001
non-commit under refs/heads/.

141
00:10:10,341 --> 00:10:14,764
The last line is the 🔴 command, git
update-ref -d. The five answers.

142
00:10:14,804 --> 00:10:18,374
It deletes the ref and its
reflog, with no merge check.

143
00:10:18,414 --> 00:10:23,887
It destroys the safety net for that ref.
Preview: git rev-parse on the ref, and note

144
00:10:23,927 --> 00:10:29,268
the ID. Recover: git update-ref with that
ID, which the reflog of HEAD or git fsck

145
00:10:29,308 --> 00:10:34,650
find. Appropriate: for a ref you created
with plumbing and have just inspected, as

146
00:10:34,690 --> 00:10:34,978
here.

147
00:10:35,318 --> 00:10:38,843
On the graph the name is gone,
and every commit is where it was.

148
00:10:39,183 --> 00:10:44,375
A symbolic ref is a name for a name. With
HEAD detached, the file holds an object

149
00:10:44,415 --> 00:10:48,897
ID, and git symbolic-ref HEAD fails:
"ref HEAD is not a symbolic ref".

150
00:10:49,237 --> 00:10:50,348
Reflog storage.

151
00:10:50,688 --> 00:10:56,203
A quick quiz. After the branch is deleted,
is its log file still there, or gone with

152
00:10:56,243 --> 00:10:57,261
it? Your answer?

153
00:11:02,101 --> 00:11:05,498
It's gone. A deleted branch
loses its reflog with it.

154
00:11:05,538 --> 00:11:09,973
Tags have none. refs/stash has a
log, and that log is the stash list.

155
00:11:10,313 --> 00:11:12,132
Now the hook, and that missing file.

156
00:11:12,472 --> 00:11:17,571
After the commit: where is main stored,
and what does the line in packed-refs say?

157
00:11:17,611 --> 00:11:19,577
Make your prediction. I'll wait.

158
00:11:27,417 --> 00:11:32,073
After packing, the only file left is the
symbolic ref, which can't be packed.

159
00:11:32,113 --> 00:11:37,013
The header lists the traits of the file.
An annotated tag is followed by a ^ line

160
00:11:37,053 --> 00:11:42,136
with the commit it points at. After the
commit, the loose file is back and wins: git

161
00:11:42,176 --> 00:11:47,014
rev-parse main prints the new ID, while
the packed-refs line still shows the old

162
00:11:47,054 --> 00:11:47,258
one.

163
00:11:47,598 --> 00:11:53,200
In the picture, main has moved to a8eea18.
The stale line still says 0c2cf43, and

164
00:11:53,240 --> 00:11:54,593
the loose file wins.

165
00:11:54,933 --> 00:11:59,334
Read the root-cause line of the box:
the scripts read one of several storage

166
00:11:59,374 --> 00:12:01,204
locations instead of asking Git.

167
00:12:04,044 --> 00:12:09,130
One line per fetched branch: an ID, a marker
for git pull, and where it came from.

168
00:12:09,170 --> 00:12:12,381
Used as a revision, FETCH_HEAD
means its first line.

169
00:12:12,721 --> 00:12:16,119
MERGE_HEAD holds two lines,
one per branch being merged.

170
00:12:16,159 --> 00:12:20,971
That is the "different semantics": a list
of IDs where a ref holds exactly one.

171
00:12:24,311 --> 00:12:28,178
HEAD and ORIG_HEAD are listed.
The two pseudorefs aren't.

172
00:12:28,218 --> 00:12:32,291
ORIG_HEAD accepts an update like
any ref, and has no reflog.

173
00:12:32,331 --> 00:12:33,799
MERGE_HEAD is refused.

174
00:12:34,139 --> 00:12:40,445
Now the incident property. This step ends
with git gc --prune=now, 🔴: it removes

175
00:12:40,485 --> 00:12:47,030
every unreachable object at once. Preview
with git fsck --unreachable and git prune

176
00:12:47,070 --> 00:12:53,376
-n. Recover only from another clone or a
backup. It's never routine maintenance.

177
00:12:53,716 --> 00:12:58,670
A quick quiz. A teammate's commit is
named by FETCH_HEAD and by nothing else.

178
00:12:58,710 --> 00:13:02,237
Does it survive the collection:
yes or no? Your answer?

179
00:13:07,077 --> 00:13:12,134
It doesn't. The name stays behind and
names nothing: "bad object FETCH_HEAD".

180
00:13:12,174 --> 00:13:16,107
If you said yes, that's the
trap: the name looks like a ref.

181
00:13:16,147 --> 00:13:20,277
Give such a commit a ref before
you rely on it: git fetch <url>

182
00:13:20,317 --> 00:13:25,375
<branch>:refs/heads/review/<name>, or
git switch -c review/<name> FETCH_HEAD.

183
00:13:36,215 --> 00:13:41,323
The lab visits every special name through
a fetch, a reset, a merge, a cherry-pick,

184
00:13:41,363 --> 00:13:46,160
a revert, a rebase, a bisect and a stash.
At the checkpoint no operation is in

185
00:13:46,200 --> 00:13:50,750
progress and three leftovers remain:
FETCH_HEAD, ORIG_HEAD and AUTO_MERGE.

186
00:13:50,790 --> 00:13:55,650
None of them describes unfinished work.
The failure and the recovery are yours.

187
00:13:57,490 --> 00:13:59,138
Five mistakes to watch for.

188
00:13:59,478 --> 00:14:05,370
Reading .git/refs/heads/<branch> in a
script. Root cause: the ref may be a line in

189
00:14:05,410 --> 00:14:11,375
packed-refs, so the script reads one of
several storage locations instead of asking

190
00:14:11,415 --> 00:14:11,664
Git.

191
00:14:12,004 --> 00:14:16,990
Grepping packed-refs for a branch. Root
cause: a later update writes a loose file

192
00:14:17,030 --> 00:14:18,851
and leaves the old line stale.

193
00:14:19,191 --> 00:14:22,393
Relying on FETCH_HEAD or
ORIG_HEAD to keep a commit.

194
00:14:22,433 --> 00:14:27,441
Root cause: apart from HEAD, root refs and
pseudorefs are not starting points for

195
00:14:27,481 --> 00:14:28,252
reachability.

196
00:14:28,592 --> 00:14:32,239
Testing for a file to ask
"is a merge in progress?".

197
00:14:32,279 --> 00:14:37,770
Root cause: root refs are not files in
every format; resolve the name with git

198
00:14:37,810 --> 00:14:40,465
rev-parse --verify --quiet MERGE_HEAD.

199
00:14:40,805 --> 00:14:46,558
Expecting a deleted branch's reflog to
remain. Root cause: deleting a branch deletes

200
00:14:46,598 --> 00:14:47,110
its log.

201
00:14:48,950 --> 00:14:55,155
Now, out of the lab. Picture a platform
team that lists stale branches for a weekly

202
00:14:55,195 --> 00:15:01,399
clean-up report. Their first script walked
.git/refs/heads and missed most branches

203
00:15:01,439 --> 00:15:07,343
on the build server, where refs are packed.
The replacement is one command: git

